<Page
	title="Editorial Policy"
	description="How SpaceNews reports: story selection, sourcing standards, verification steps, launch coverage rules, and how our articles are made."
	eyebrow="How We Report"
	updated="November 8, 2026"
>
	<p>
		This page sets out the standards every SpaceNews article is published under. It is the same
		checklist we work to internally, published so readers can hold us to it. If you believe an
		article falls short of these standards, <a href="/contact">contact us</a> — and if it contains
		an error, our <a href="/corrections">Corrections page</a> explains what happens next.
	</p>

	<h2>1. Story Selection</h2>
	<p>
		We cover missions, technology, astronomy, and exploration. A story qualifies for coverage when
		it has a verifiable core: a confirmed event, an official announcement, a peer-reviewed result,
		or a scheduled event with an official source. In practice that means:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Confirmed events are reported as news; scheduled events are reported as previews and are clearly labelled as not yet having happened.</li>
		<li>Every preview is followed by result coverage once the outcome is known — including when the outcome is a delay, a scrub, or a failure.</li>
		<li>Claims that rest only on anonymous posts or unsourced reports are not published as fact. If we mention them at all, they are labelled unconfirmed and attributed.</li>
		<li>Background explainers are published to add context — programme history, technical background, prior results — not to pad out the news list.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>2. Sourcing Standards</h2>
	<p>
		Sources are ranked, and articles are built from the highest rank available:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Primary sources first</strong> — space agency and launch provider mission pages,
			official statements and press releases, peer-reviewed papers, and published mission data.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Reputable specialist reporting</strong> may supplement primary sources for colour
			and context, and is attributed when it contributes a specific fact.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Trackers and analysts</strong> (orbital data, launch manifests, independent
			experts) are cited as what they are: assessment and analysis, never as official
			confirmation.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Every article carries a <strong>Sources</strong> section linking to the material it is based
		on. We never invent a source, a quotation, a date, or a figure, and direct quotations are kept
		short and attributed to the person or organisation that said them.
	</p>

	<h2>3. Verification Before Publication</h2>
	<p>Before an article is published, its key claims are checked against the cited sources:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Dates, times, and time zones are confirmed against the official schedule or announcement — and re-checked on the day for launch coverage, because schedules move.</li>
		<li>Figures that carry the story (masses, distances, durations, counts) are traced to the source that published them, not copied from secondary summaries.</li>
		<li>Headlines, summaries, and image captions are held to the same accuracy standard as the article body — a headline may not claim more than the reporting supports.</li>
		<li>If a central fact cannot be verified, the article is held, rewritten to reflect what is known, or not published.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>4. Launch Coverage Rules</h2>
	<p>Launch reporting follows stricter rules because times and outcomes change quickly:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>A launch window is reported as a window. An exact liftoff time is stated only after it is confirmed.</li>
		<li>Result articles are written only after liftoff is confirmed by the provider or a reliable tracker — never from the schedule alone.</li>
		<li>For classified missions, we report only what officials confirm. Payload counts, deployment details, and mission purposes that have not been officially confirmed are identified as analyst assessment or left out.</li>
		<li>When a launch is delayed or scrubbed, the change is reported in follow-up coverage rather than silently edited into an earlier preview.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>5. How Our Articles Are Made</h2>
	<p>
		SpaceNews uses modern research and drafting tools, including AI-assisted tools, in its
		production process. The tools operate inside the standards on this page, which are set and
		owned by the publisher: topics must pass the story-selection test, drafting is grounded in
		retrieved source material, and the verification steps in section 3 are a publication
		requirement, not an option. Articles are published under the SpaceNews name; we do not create
		fictional author profiles or attribute work to people who did not do it.
	</p>

	<h2>6. Independence and Conflicts of Interest</h2>
	<p>
		SpaceNews is independent: it is not owned by or affiliated with any space agency, launch
		provider, or aerospace company. Advertising funds the site but never influences story
		selection, wording, or conclusions, and advertisers do not see articles before publication.
		We do not publish paid or sponsored content disguised as news; any future sponsored material
		will be labelled as advertising.
	</p>

	<h2>7. Images</h2>
	<p>
		Article photography is illustrative and is sourced from
		<a href="https://unsplash.com" rel="noopener">Unsplash</a> under its licence unless otherwise
		credited. Captions identify images as illustrations where they do not depict the specific
		mission, vehicle, or event described.
	</p>

	<h2>8. Corrections and Updates</h2>
	<p>
		Developing stories are updated as events unfold, and updates are distinguished from
		corrections: an update adds new information; a correction fixes something we got wrong.
		Significant corrections are noted on the article itself. The full process is on our
		<a href="/corrections">Corrections page</a>.
	</p>
</Page>
